1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,528
Hello, fellow aliens.

2
00:00:01,935 --> 00:00:08,875
This is not an episode of Earthlings 101, the crash course for alien visitors, but a video response to an Earthling video about a spinning disk.

3
00:00:09,138 --> 00:00:13,224
As most of you are used to voyage inside spinning disks, you might find this interesting.

4
00:00:13,528 --> 00:00:17,436
The Earthling video shows a disk with an eccentric hole, spinning vertically on the ground.

5
00:00:17,680 --> 00:00:21,848
The funny thing is that the heavy side of the disk turns upwards, seemingly defying gravity.

6
00:00:22,253 --> 00:00:23,213
Why does this happen?

7
00:00:23,833 --> 00:00:29,842
Now, before you try this for yourself, if you have one of those fancy frictionless piloting consoles, it won't work there.

8
00:00:30,326 --> 00:00:33,762
Better try on a rough surface, as friction plays an important role in the effect.

9
00:00:34,385 --> 00:00:38,148
Actually, this effect is based on the three basic rules of saucer physics.

10
00:00:38,809 --> 00:00:42,449
For those of you who don't remember their saucer piloting lessons, here a quick reminder.

11
00:00:42,929 --> 00:00:47,160
The first rule of saucer physics says that a saucer will always spin around its center of mass.

12
00:00:47,545 --> 00:00:54,860
So, if you abduct a herd of cows, and store them all on one side of the saucer, the spinning axis will be somewhere between the geometrical center and the cows.

13
00:00:55,323 --> 00:00:58,181
That's why you always check your cargo distribution before lifting off.

14
00:00:58,489 --> 00:01:04,249
The second rule of saucer physics says that when the saucer scrapes horizontally over the ground, it will have the tendency to tip over.

15
00:01:04,589 --> 00:01:06,181
That's why you always land vertically.

16
00:01:06,670 --> 00:01:13,430
And finally, the third rule of saucer physics says that when you incline the spinning saucer into one direction, it will have the tendency to roll sidewards.

17
00:01:13,850 --> 00:01:17,189
This is called precession, and every saucer pilot is painfully aware of it.

18
00:01:17,519 --> 00:01:19,959
This is why you stop spinning before you maneuver or land.

19
00:01:20,719 --> 00:01:25,091
Now, imagine a saucer pilot who had too much Sagittarian brandies, so he becomes careless.

20
00:01:25,598 --> 00:01:30,342
He lands on Earth with a herd of cows on one side of the saucer, and he forgets to extend the landing gear.

21
00:01:30,849 --> 00:01:32,149
What happens when he lands?

22
00:01:32,629 --> 00:01:36,129
Well, the point where the saucer will touch the ground will not be below the center of mass.

23
00:01:36,597 --> 00:01:39,402
If the saucer is not spinning, the saucer will just tip over.

24
00:01:39,729 --> 00:01:41,588
But now suppose that the saucer is spinning.

25
00:01:41,981 --> 00:01:45,461
The first rule of saucer physics says that it will spin around its center of mass.

26
00:01:45,941 --> 00:01:50,181
That means, when it lands, the contact point with the ground will scrape over the ground in a circle.

27
00:01:50,641 --> 00:02:01,901
Now, the second rule says that the friction on the ground will make the saucer tip, and the third rule says that this tipping moment will create a precession effect which makes the saucer turn sidewards instead of tipping, in this case such that the center of mass goes upwards.

28
00:02:02,441 --> 00:02:06,529
So, the cows will move upwards, not downwards, but back to the spinning disc.

29
00:02:06,752 --> 00:02:08,075
It's basically the same thing.

30
00:02:08,268 --> 00:02:11,656
Only without the cows and the saucer, the disc rotates around its center of mass.

31
00:02:11,950 --> 00:02:22,067
So, unless it's in a perfectly symmetrical position, the lowest point scrapes in a circle over the ground, which creates a tipping torque, which is just what the precession effect was waiting for, to turn the heavy side of the disc upwards.

32
00:02:22,388 --> 00:02:26,252
This happens continuously until the center of mass is above the contact point with the ground.

33
00:02:26,778 --> 00:02:30,922
By the way, humans have a toy called tippy top which basically works the same way.

34
00:02:31,428 --> 00:02:39,489
That's, in a nutshell, the reason why A, the disc turns heavy side up, and B, why saucer pilots should go easy on sagittarian brandy.

35
00:02:39,489 --> 00:02:40,686
Thanks for watching.

